<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · text</p><h1 class="enc-title">Luther Bible</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1522">1522</a>–<a href="/years/1534">1534</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/16th-century">16th century</a> · Also known as Luther's Bible</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Luther Bible is <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/martin-luther">Martin Luther</a>'s translation of the Bible into German, from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hebrew">Hebrew</a> and Greek, with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-testament">New Testament</a> published in 1522 and the complete Bible in 1534. Widely printed and read, it shaped the standard written German language and served as the main Bible of Lutheran churches. Its success encouraged other vernacular translations, including the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zurich-bible">Zürich Bible</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/luther-bible.jpg" alt="Luther Bible" width="320" height="242" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Torsten Schleese · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Lutherbibel.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The Luther Bible was not the first German Bible. About 1,000 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manuscript">manuscripts</a> or fragments of medieval German translations survive, mainly from the thirteenth and fourteenth centuries, and by 1522 there had been at least eighteen complete printed German Bibles, ninety editions of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gospels">Gospels</a> and Sunday readings, and some fourteen German Psalters. These earlier translations followed the Latin Vulgate word for word and were often clumsy. Luther instead aimed at idiomatic Saxon German, translating phrase by phrase.</p><p>Luther prepared his <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-testament">New Testament</a> during ten months at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wartburg">Wartburg Castle</a>, from 4 May 1521 to 3 March 1522, producing a first version in eleven weeks. A textual basis was the edition of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/erasmus">Erasmus of Rotterdam</a>, the Novum Testamentum omne of 1519, with its Greek text and annotations. The New Testament was printed in September 1522, the so-called September Bible. The complete Bible appeared in 1534 as a collaboration of Luther with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/johannes-bugenhagen">Johannes Bugenhagen</a>, Justus Jonas, Caspar Creuziger, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/philip-melanchthon">Philip Melanchthon</a>, Matthäus Aurogallus and Georg Rörer. He kept refining it until his death in 1546.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Contents</h2><p>The 1534 Bible contained 75 books: 39 of the Old Testament, nine of the Apocrypha and 27 of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-testament">New Testament</a>. The Hans Lufft press in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wittenberg">Wittenberg</a> printed it on 1,824 pages in two volumes with 117 woodcuts. Luther placed the apocryphal books between the Testaments under the title Apocrypha: These Books Are Not Held Equal to the Scriptures, but Are Useful and Good to Read. He moved <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hebrew">Hebrews</a>, James, Jude and Revelation to the end of the New Testament, calling James an epistle of straw.</p><p>The translation was controversial. Luther added the word alone to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-empire">Romans</a> 3:28, so that man is justified alone through faith, though the Greek has no such word. He defended it as required by German idiom and by Paul's meaning, and the 2017 revision keeps the word with a footnote giving a literal rendering.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reception and legacy</h2><p>About 200,000 copies in hundreds of editions appeared before Luther died. An unbound copy cost about a month's wages for an average labourer, so churches, pastors and schools were the main buyers. Hans Lufft alone printed over 100,000 copies between 1534 and 1574. The translation, based largely on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saxons">Saxon</a> chancery language, helped the emergence of a standard German. Catholic rivals answered with their own versions, such as Hieronymus Emser's revision in 1526, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zurich-bible">Zürich Bible</a> of 1525 to 1530 revised Luther's New Testament.</p><p>Luther's text influenced the English translations of William Tyndale and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/myles-coverdale">Myles Coverdale</a>, and was the basis for Bibles in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/scandinavia">Scandinavia</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/denmark">Denmark</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/norway">Norway</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/finland">Finland</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-the-netherlands">Netherlands</a>. The Evangelical Church in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a> carried out official revisions in 1892, 1912, 1984 and 2017.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Luther Bible” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The Lutheran Reformation was first institutionalised in the Saxon visitations from 1527, which became a model for Protestant churches, and the language of the Saxon chancery, used in <b>Luther’s Bible</b>, contributed greatly to the formation of standard written German.</blockquote><a href="/empire/electorate-of-saxony">Electorate of Saxony · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote><b>Luther’s Bible</b> helped to form standard written German, and translations such as the Welsh Bible of 1588 and the King James Version of 1611 became classics of their languages.</blockquote><a href="/movement/protestant-reformation">Protestant Reformation · Legacy</a></li></ul></section>
